<p><strong>Natural vs artificial:</strong> the style is agnostic, but honest. A laminate should look like a good laminate, not imitate marble; a quartz should be plain or subtly veined, not a printed drama slab. The one upgrade we push at every budget: <strong>anti-fingerprint super-matte on any surface hands touch daily</strong> — it's the difference between a kitchen that looks new at year five and one that looks tired at year one. The ply-grade logic underneath it all is covered in our <a href="/blogs/calibrated-vs-normal-plywood/">plywood guide</a>.</p>
<p>Section 06</p>
<h2>Furniture Style: Low, Flush, and Mostly Built-In</h2>
<p>Minimalist furniture follows four rules: <strong>low profiles, straight or barely-eased silhouettes, visual lightness, and built-in beats loose</strong>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sofa:</strong> straight arms or armless, seat around 400–420mm, in the secondary tone. Skip chaise-lounge sprawl in compact rooms; a bench-cushion two-seater plus one accent chair reads cleaner than a bulky L.</li>
  <li><strong>Dining:</strong> slim-leg or single-pedestal table; a bench on one side removes four chair-leg verticals from the room at a stroke.</li>
  <li><strong>TV unit:</strong> a single floating slab, 300–400mm off the floor, full wall width. The float matters — visible floor under furniture is the cheapest square footage you'll ever "add."</li>
  <li><strong>Coffee table:</strong> one low piece, solid form or thin plate; nesting tables if you entertain.</li>
  <li><strong>Beds:</strong> low platform, flat or lightly upholstered plain headboard; storage via side drawers rather than hydraulic lift where ceiling heights are standard, because the lower profile is the point.</li>
  <li><strong>Wardrobes &amp; storage:</strong> floor-to-ceiling, handle-less, flush — designed as architecture. Lofts integrated behind the same shutter line, not stacked as visible boxes.</li>
  <li><strong>Floating everything possible:</strong> vanities, consoles, bedside ledges — every floated unit returns floor to the eye and makes robot-vacuum cleaning genuinely one-pass.</li>
  <li><strong>Modular logic:</strong> standard module widths (450/600/900mm) keep costs sane and repairs replaceable; custom widths are reserved for the one wall that must be perfect.</li>
</ul>
<p>Section 07</p>
<h2>Minimalism Room by Room: Build Specs</h2>
<h3>Kitchen</h3>
<p>Slab shutters, handle-less — either Gola/J-profile channels or push-to-open on uppers. One finish for all base and wall units; the tall-unit bank may go one shade deeper. Backsplash is where minimalist kitchens are won: <strong>run the countertop quartz up the wall</strong> or use one large-format plain tile — a busy backsplash undoes every flush shutter around it. Appliances integrate behind panels wherever budget allows; the chimney is a concealed or slim linear model, never a decorative pyramid. Corner units get LeMans or magic-corner internals, tall units get pantry pull-outs — because a minimalist counter is only possible when the storage behind it works hard. An island earns its place only above roughly 12×10ft of kitchen; below that, a breakfast ledge off the counter does the job without choking circulation. Our <a href="/blogs/kitchen-layouts-guide/">kitchen layouts guide</a> and <a href="/blogs/modular-kitchen-storage-ideas/">storage internals guide</a> go deeper on both decisions.</p>
<h3>Bedroom</h3>
<p>The wardrobe wall <em>is</em> the room: one flush run, floor to ceiling, in one finish — this single decision delivers more calm than everything else combined. The bed sits low; bedside pendants hang instead of table lamps, freeing the side ledges to stay empty. The dressing unit folds into the wardrobe line (a shutter-integrated mirror) rather than standing alone. Soft furnishing follows the two-texture rule: one bed fabric, one throw, done.</p>
<h3>Living room</h3>
<p>One feature texture maximum — a fluted panel, a limewash wall or a stone-look porcelain slab behind the TV — and everything else stays in the base tone. The false ceiling holds a plain peripheral drop with a concealed cove; the shadow-gap ceiling detail (a 10–12mm negative line where ceiling meets wall, replacing the cornice) is the single detail that most says "designed, not decorated." Display storage is 80% closed, 20% open, and the open fifth holds five objects, not fifteen. Curtains: single-color, full-height, track concealed in the ceiling drop.</p>
<h3>Dining</h3>
<p>One pendant line over the table centred and dimmable; a flush crockery wall rather than a standalone hutch. If the dining wall carries anything, it's one large artwork — the style's entire décor allowance for the zone.</p>
<h3>Bathroom</h3>
<p>Large-format tiles floor and wall (fewer grout lines, faster cleaning), wall-hung vanity in BWP ply with a matte front, mirror cabinet instead of mirror-plus-shelf, concealed cistern, and fittings in one metal finish. Accessories mount inside the shower niche, not on the walls. This is also where minimalism is most practical: fewer edges is fewer places for Bangalore's hard water to leave its mark.</p>
