<p>Section 08</p>
<h2>Lighting Design: See the Light, Not the Fixture</h2>
<p><strong>Natural light is the first fixture.</strong> Minimalism maximises it: sheer single-track curtains, no valances or pelmets, and furniture kept below sill lines so windows read full-size. In Bangalore's strong sun, add a lined second track in bedrooms for sleep — the double-track detail disappears into the ceiling drop.</p>
<p>Artificial light works in four layers, mostly hidden:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ambient:</strong> concealed cove in the ceiling periphery plus a sparse grid of trimless or slim-trim recessed downlights. Fewer, better-placed fittings beat the builder-standard "one downlight per square metre" grid.</li>
  <li><strong>Task:</strong> under-cabinet strips in the kitchen (4000K here only), a focused reading pendant per bedside, mirror-integrated light at vanities.</li>
  <li><strong>Accent:</strong> one wall-washer or in-shelf strip on the single feature the room owns — light the texture, not the room.</li>
  <li><strong>Decorative:</strong> one statement pendant per home zone maximum, chosen as sculpture: dining pendant, or a living floor lamp — not both fighting.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Kelvin discipline:</strong> 2700–3000K everywhere people rest, 4000K over work zones, and nothing above. Put living, dining and master-bedroom circuits on dimmers — in a style with no visual noise, light level is your only mood control, and it's the cheapest luxury in the whole BOQ at roughly ₹1,500–3,000 per dimmer point.</p>
<p>Section 09</p>
<h2>Decorative Elements: The Short, Strict List</h2>
<p>Minimalist décor follows one law: <strong>fewer, larger, intentional.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Plants:</strong> one to three large statement plants (rubber plant, fiddle-leaf, areca) in plain pots — never a windowsill army of small ones.</li>
  <li><strong>Wall art:</strong> one oversized piece per wall that gets art at all; gallery walls belong to other styles.</li>
  <li><strong>Curtains:</strong> solid color, full height, puddle-free; the curtain is a plane, not a pattern.</li>
  <li><strong>Carpets:</strong> one large plain or subtly textured rug that sits under all front furniture legs — a small rug floating in the middle of a room reads like a mistake.</li>
  <li><strong>Artifacts &amp; décor pieces:</strong> the five-object rule per open surface zone — a vessel, a book stack, a sculptural piece. Rotate seasonally rather than adding.</li>
  <li><strong>Metal accents:</strong> one finish across the home (all black, or all brushed brass) — mixed metals are a layering device this style declines.</li>
  <li><strong>Books:</strong> behind glass or closed shutters, with one styled open shelf if reading is your identity.</li>
  <li><strong>Mirrors:</strong> the style's favourite trick — one large frameless or slim-framed mirror opposite or beside a window doubles perceived light and depth for a few thousand rupees.</li>
  <li><strong>Texture:</strong> where color is restricted, texture does the emotional work — boucle, linen weave, fluted wood, honed stone. A room can be one color and still be rich if it's four textures.</li>
</ul>
