<p><strong>Room sizes:</strong> small homes gain the most (the style manufactures perceived space); medium homes get balance; large homes and open floor plans need one caution — vast minimalist rooms can drift toward showroom emptiness, so we zone them with rugs, ceiling drops and texture changes rather than partitions.</p>
<h3>The buyer profile, honestly drawn</h3>
<p>Across our order book the style clusters at ages 27–45, mid-to-high income, working professionals and DINK couples, plus a steady stream of NRIs furnishing Bangalore homes they visit twice a year (minimalism's low upkeep suits the empty months). Retired couples increasingly choose it for the same reason hotels do: nothing to trip on, everything reachable, cleaning trivial. <strong>Families with kids under eight</strong> should choose warm minimalism with tougher finishes and oversized toy storage — or a more forgiving style; we say this in the first meeting, not after handover. <strong>Pet owners:</strong> mid-tone floors (fur shows on both extremes), performance fabrics, and no floating shelves at tail height. <strong>Maximalist personalities</strong> — collectors, display-lovers, festival-decorators — will fight this home daily and should not buy it, however good the photos look.</p>
